# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# WHY: core.autocrlf is true on the Windows development machine, so without
# this a fresh clone converts these files to CRLF -- and a CRLF shell script
# does not run under WSL bash, it dies on the shebang or on a stray carriage
# return inside a here-document. The scripts in test/ are invoked precisely
# that way (`wsl.exe -d Ubuntu -- bash .../test/verilator_trace.sh`), so the
# breakage would appear only after a re-checkout, on a tree that had always
# worked, and would look like a WSL or toolchain problem.
#
# The files are already STORED with LF; this pins the checkout side too.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Program images, too (Wilson). They are STORED with LF and every generator
# writes LF (`newline="\n"`), but core.autocrlf checked them out as CRLF -- so
# regenerating an image (make_smoke_image --all, corpus.py) flipped every line
# ending of a byte-identical file and showed it as modified. Pinned to LF on
# checkout, a regeneration that changes nothing now shows nothing.
*.hex text eol=lf

# Python -- the models, the gates and every frozen-hash check (Wilson 10-30).
# A check that hashes a file's bytes saw CRLF here and LF on CI: the frozen
# stage-0 model (e55d0b63 on Windows, f19f2177 on CI, the same text) made the
# gate refuse to run on CI only. The checks now normalise CRLF -> LF; this pins
# the checkout too, so both platforms hold the same bytes.
*.py text eol=lf
